WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.141
EtherNet/IP and PROFINET are prominent industrial Ethernet technologies, but they are not simply ordinary office applications placed on a plant network.

00:00:11.141 --> 00:00:22.281
They support device discovery, configuration, diagnostics, cyclic control data, and other operations whose timing and availability may be important to production.

00:00:22.281 --> 00:00:29.956
EtherNet/IP uses the Common Industrial Protocol, or CIP, across standard Ethernet and IP networking.

00:00:29.956 --> 00:00:40.106
It can carry recurring input and output data as well as explicit messages for configuration, status, and other services.

00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:47.286
The expected device roles and connection patterns depend on the engineered control solution.

00:00:47.286 --> 00:00:54.960
PROFINET supports communication among controllers, distributed input and output, drives, and related automation equipment.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:02.140
Different communication classes can serve ordinary supervisory functions and more demanding real-time control.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:09.815
Engineering and discovery functions are useful operationally but should remain within approved administrative pathways.

00:01:09.815 --> 00:01:14.023
Both protocols exist inside broader vendor ecosystems.

00:01:14.023 --> 00:01:23.678
Controllers, safety systems, HMIs, drives, engineering suites, firmware, device profiles, and management tools are designed to work together.

00:01:23.678 --> 00:01:32.343
Security teams need vendor and site documentation because product versions, optional protections, and supported configurations vary.

00:01:32.343 --> 00:01:35.562
Real-time requirements shape defensive work.

00:01:35.562 --> 00:01:44.227
Unplanned scanning, inline controls, or configuration changes can introduce latency, device stress, or loss of communication.

00:01:44.227 --> 00:01:51.901
Assess changes in a representative environment and coordinate deployment with control engineers and vendors.

00:01:51.901 --> 00:02:08.488
Protocol security often depends on architecture and administration: segment control zones, restrict engineering tools, control discovery and management paths, secure remote access, preserve known-good configurations, and monitor unexpected devices or role changes.

00:02:08.488 --> 00:02:16.658
Protocol awareness identifies behavior; engineering context determines whether that behavior is necessary, approved, and safe.
